      facts: {"release":"Fearless","releaseDate":"2008-11-11","writers":["Taylor Swift"],"producers":["Nathan Chapman","Taylor Swift"],"isSingle":true,"singleReleaseDate":"2008-09-12","themes":["forbidden love","fairy-tale rewrites","defiance"]},
      dossier: {"whyItMatters":["The song that turned a country teenager into a global pop star. Swift has said she wrote it alone on her bedroom floor in about 20 minutes, reaching for the most famous forbidden-love story she knew — Romeo and Juliet — and overruling Shakespeare to hand the couple the happy ending the play denies them. Released as Fearless's lead single on Sept. 15, 2008, it became the album's calling card and the template for the fairy-tale-with-a-rewrite that would define her early writing.","It was a genuine crossover event, not just a country hit: it peaked at No. 4 on the Billboard Hot 100, became the first country song ever to reach No. 1 on Billboard's Pop Songs (Mainstream Top 40) airplay chart, and is certified 8× platinum by the RIAA. Thirteen years later its re-recording became a landmark of a different kind — the first song of the entire Taylor's Version project and proof the strategy could top the charts on its own."],"meaning":{"confirmed":["Written solo by Swift and produced by Nathan Chapman and Swift; she has said it started with a boy her family disapproved of and that she wrote it in roughly 20 minutes on her bedroom floor, recasting the romance through Romeo and Juliet but replacing the tragic ending with a proposal.","Released Sept. 15, 2008 as the lead single from Fearless on Big Machine Records; it debuted at No. 25 on Hot Country Songs — her highest debut on that chart at the time — and reached No. 1 there in November 2008, her third country No. 1.","It peaked at No. 4 on the Billboard Hot 100 and became the first country song to top Billboard's Pop Songs airplay chart, and the RIAA has since certified it 8× platinum.","\"Love Story (Taylor's Version)\" was released Feb. 12, 2021 as the first single from her re-recording campaign; it debuted at No. 1 on Hot Country Songs (her first song to debut at the summit there) and at No. 11 on the Hot 100, making Swift the second artist after Dolly Parton to top Hot Country Songs with both an original and its re-recording."],"supported":["The single's success is widely credited as the moment Swift's audience expanded decisively beyond country radio into mainstream pop, setting up the album-of-the-year run that followed.","Its Renaissance-costume period video became one of her most-recognized early visuals and a recurring reference point in her later fairy-tale imagery."],"fanTheories":["Because Swift has framed the song as a fantasy built on a disapproved-of crush rather than a documented romance, fans generally read \"Love Story\" as an idealized rewrite — she takes a real feeling of being told no and imagines the version where the story is allowed to end happily."]},"connections":[{"relatedId":"song:you-belong-with-me","label":"You Belong With Me","why":"The two blockbuster singles that carried Fearless's country-to-pop crossover — \"Love Story\" the fairy-tale fantasy, \"You Belong With Me\" the girl-next-door underdog, together the era's commercial engine."},{"relatedId":"song:white-horse","label":"White Horse","why":"The morning-after rebuttal to this song's fantasy: where \"Love Story\" rewrites the ending happy, \"White Horse\" admits the fairy tale was a lie — the album arguing with itself about romance."},{"relatedId":"song:fearless","label":"Fearless","why":"The album's two definitive fairy-tale statements — the title track turns a first date into a leap of faith, \"Love Story\" turns a tragedy into a proposal."}],"sources":[{"name":"Love Story (Taylor Swift song) — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Love_Story_(Taylor_Swift_song)","reliability":2},{"name":"Taylor Swift's 'Love Story (Taylor's Version)' Debuts at No. 1 on Hot Country Songs — Billboard","url":"https://www.billboard.com/pro/taylor-swift-love-story-number-one-hot-country-songs/","reliability":4}]},
